{"author":{"id":"746a4d30afc20b78119cb8ed7c194f988174fc252f0e9d9eb92022244ef86db3"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1779504099,"nonce":"eWIigfc275a6ZQnUn6KJaBNKN1k=","metadata":{"github-id":"I_kwDOK4fCvM8AAAABDJ0COg","github-url":"https://github.com/rolker/unh_echoboats_project11/issues/164","origin":"github"},"title":"CrabbingPathFollower undulates on SE-bound lines only — asymmetric behavior","message":"## Symptom\n\nDuring [#160](https://github.com/rolker/unh_echoboats_project11/issues/160) (2026-05-22 deployment), the survey path follower (`marine_nav_crabbing_path_follower::CrabbingPathFollower`) exhibited **asymmetric line-following behavior**:\n\n- **NW-bound lines**: clean, near-zero cross-track error\n- **SE-bound lines**: noticeable undulation around the trackline\n\nOperator confirmed in real time: \"Lines are much straighter going NW than SE.\"\n\nThe asymmetry persisted across multiple survey-loop repeats at the same site under the same wind/current conditions, so it's not a one-off transient.\n\n## Candidate causes\n\n1. **Asymmetric controller response to lateral disturbance** — port crab (the response needed to hold an SE line under today's wind/current) noisier than starboard crab. Could be PID gain asymmetry, deadband, or integral-windup difference between port-side vs. starboard-side correction.\n2. **Asymmetric thruster authority** — port and starboard thrusters not delivering equal force per unit of commanded value (mechanical, electrical, or thruster-config asymmetry). This would also produce direction-of-travel-dependent behavior.\n3. **Wind/current alignment with NW lines** — if the disturbance was predominantly along the NW axis, NW-bound lines benefit from natural alignment while SE-bound lines fight the disturbance. (Today's winds were light; tide easing past flood toward slack — disturbances modest.)\n4. **Battery-state-dependent thruster authority** — at low SOC the thrusters may saturate asymmetrically (one closer to its compensation limit than the other).\n\n## Diagnosis (post-processing)\n\nBag analysis from #160's in-water bag(s):\n\n1. **XTE per direction**: bin per-line cross-track error by line bearing; compare NW-bound vs. SE-bound distributions.\n2. **`cmd_vel.linear.y` sign distribution**: compare port-crab vs. starboard-crab command magnitudes. If they're symmetric in `cmd_vel` but asymmetric in resulting XTE, the issue is thruster-side, not controller-side.\n3. **Thruster command vs. actual output** if available: any per-side telemetry on actual thruster RPM/power vs. commanded? If asymmetric, that's the smoking gun.\n\n## Class-prep impact\n\nFor the June 2026 Summer Hydro class, this affects survey-quality at line spacing \u003c 2 m (1 m spacing showed the undulation most clearly). Acceptable mitigations:\n\n- Plan survey patterns with longer line spacing (≥ 2 m) until tuned.\n- Pick survey orientations aligned with the asymmetry (NW-clean direction) where mission geometry allows.\n\nA real fix requires diagnosis above. Priority: medium (not class-blocking — workarounds exist).\n\n## Context\n\n- Originating deployment: #160 (2026-05-22)\n- Dev log: `docs/logs/2026/2026-05-22_dev_logs.md` — \"Asymmetry confirmed\" entry + \"Conditions check + SE-undulation hypothesis\"\n- Bags: see #160 wrap-up for paths\n\nPart of #160.\n\n---\n**Authored-By**: `Claude Code Agent`\n**Model**: `Claude Opus 4.7 (1M context)`","files":null}]}